@page "/build/sheet"
@implements IDisposable
@inject CharacterSession Session

@* The sheet on its own, with every name on it saying what it means.

   **The descriptions were in `data/rules` the whole time with nothing on the sheet showing them.**
   A printed sheet says "Presence 6d" and "Plot Hook" and "TECH POWERS" and leaves a player to know
   what those are; every one of them has a verified sentence behind it in the rules data, and until
   now only the editors ever showed one.

   **It is its own address rather than a mode on the review step, because the printed sheet is the
   deliverable.** Review is where a character is checked and exported, and its sheet is the one
   about to go on paper; turning forty names on it into controls would change the document. So the
   explanations live on a page somebody asks for, and `SheetView` draws them only when asked —
   which is `Explain`, off everywhere else.

   **It is under `/build`, so it wears the builder's chrome.** This is the sheet of the character
   being built, not a demonstration and not a reference; a reader arriving here is mid-job and the
   step list is where they go next. *@

<PageTitle>@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Session.Sheet.Name) ? "Unnamed character" : Session.Sheet.Name) — Prowlers &amp; Paragons</PageTitle>

<h1>The sheet, explained</h1>
<p class="muted">Every name here says what it is. Hover it, or tab to it.</p>

<SheetView Explain="true" />

<StepButtons Back="build/review" BackLabel="GM review" />

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Redraw when the character changes. <c>SheetView</c> subscribes for itself, but the title
    /// above is this page's and would otherwise keep the name the character had on arrival.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized() => Session.Changed += StateHasChanged;

    public void Dispose() => Session.Changed -= StateHasChanged;
}
